West Virginia Coal Mining
The Environmental and Economic Legacy of Coal Mining in West Virginia: Globalization, Uneven Development, and Local Resilience
This is a portfolio copy of a GEOG 101 term project at Oregon State University. The public version is an ArcGIS StoryMap:
[ArcGIS Map](https://arcg.is/1CTyrn3)
What this project is
This project looks at the long-term environmental and economic legacy of coal mining in West Virginia. It treats the state’s coal history as a case of extractive globalization: companies removed the resource and most of the profit, then left communities with contaminated water, abandoned mines, job loss, and limited options for recovery.
What it covers
The StoryMap is organized around three connected findings:

Environmental persistence
Acid mine drainage and other mining impacts do not disappear quickly. Water-quality problems can last for decades, especially in abandoned underground mines and mining-influenced streams.
Economic restructuring
Mining and logging employment collapsed from its mid-to-late 20th century peak. Other sectors, especially government and services, grew, but that shift has not fully replaced the loss of high-wage extractive jobs in many coal-dependent communities.
Limits on local resilience
Recovery is possible in some places, but it is constrained by lasting pollution, out-migration of human capital, concentrated land ownership, and weak local control over post-coal development.

